Use Case: Actively Exploited Vulnerability Affecting a High-Priority Technology
text

1. Receive threat signal.
2. Confirm the vulnerability/exploit information is credible and relevant.
3. Identify the affected product, version, and attack conditions.
4. Check authoritative internal inventory sources.
5. Determine whether the organization uses the technology.
6. Identify affected assets, exposure status, and owners.
7. Send an actionable alert to VM / Engineering.
8. Send hunt or investigation context to IR if exploitation risk warrants it.
9. Track remediation, mitigation, validation, or accepted risk.
10. Record the outcome and stakeholder feedback.

Pilot Definition of Done

    A real or test signal completed the full workflow.
    An affected-status decision was made.
    An owner received a specific action.
    The action was tracked to completion, mitigation, or documented acceptance.
    The workflow was improved based on feedback.

9. P3 — PUBLISH INTELLIGENCE
Reporting Standards

Every report should answer the “so what?” immediately.

    Explain why it matters to the reader.
    Separate observed facts from analytical judgments.
    State confidence where appropriate.
    Include source context.
    State whether the organization is confirmed affected, potentially affected, or not currently affected.
    Include a clear recommended action.
    Name the action owner.
    Tag the report to the relevant PIR and GIR.
    Avoid sending raw IOC lists with no context or action.

