━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Goal
Prioritize realistic threats to the organization — not every threat group or every technology.

★ Key Rule
Scenario first.
Actor second.

Do not start with a giant “APT list.” Start with the ways the organization could realistically be harmed.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
STARTER THREAT SCENARIOS
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Scenario 1 — Exploitation of Public-Facing Systems
Vulnerable VPN, firewall, remote-access service, web application, email server, edge device, or cloud/admin service.

Scenario 2 — Phishing, Credential Theft, and Identity Compromise
Phishing, fake login pages, password spraying, MFA abuse, stolen tokens, OAuth abuse, and vendor impersonation.

Scenario 3 — Ransomware / Access Broker Intrusion
Stolen credentials, exposed remote services, exploitation, lateral movement, data theft, encryption, and extortion.

Scenario 4 — Espionage / Sensitive Data Theft
Targeted access to email, cloud data, sensitive systems, research, operational data, legal data, or personnel data.

Scenario 5 — Third-Party / Supply-Chain Compromise
Vendor access, contractor accounts, trusted integrations, shared services, or software/service-provider compromise.

Scenario 6 — Public Disruption / Defacement / DDoS
Only include if relevant. Examples include public website disruption, hacktivism, defacement, denial-of-service, or public-service interruption.

